---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Controller from "../components/Controller.astro";
import Support from "../components/Support.astro";
import { DOWNLOAD_APK, REPO, MEASURED } from "../consts";
---

<Base>
  <section class="hero-section">
    <div class="wrap hero">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <h1>No controller?<br />Use your phone.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          PhonePad turns an Android phone into a wireless Xbox-compatible
          controller for Windows. No Bluetooth, no cable, nothing to buy.
        </p>
        <div class="cta">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href={DOWNLOAD_APK} rel="noopener">Download the app</a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/setup/">How to set it up</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="hero-art" aria-hidden="false">
        <div class="light"></div>
        <div class="phone">
          <Controller animate />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="figures">
        <span><b>{MEASURED.latency}</b> round trip</span>
        <span><b>{MEASURED.rate}</b> packets a second</span>
        <span><b>{MEASURED.loss}</b> loss over {MEASURED.packets} packets</span>
        <span><b>{MEASURED.jitter}</b> jitter</span>
      </p>
      <p class="small muted" style="margin-top:14px">
        Measured on a Galaxy S24 Ultra and a Windows 11 PC on home Wi-Fi. The
        method and the raw figures are in
        <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/benchmarks.md`} rel="noopener">the benchmarks</a>.
        Nothing on this site is a claim the repository does not support.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">Windows sees a real controller</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Not a keyboard emulator, and not a remote-desktop trick. PhonePad
        creates a virtual Xbox 360 pad on an XInput slot, so anything that takes
        a controller takes this.
      </p>
      <ul class="rows" style="margin-top:2.2rem;max-width:760px">
        <li>
          <h3>Xbox Cloud Gaming</h3>
          <p>
            Play Game Pass titles in the Xbox app or a browser with no
            controller in the house. There is one thing to know about how cloud
            clients bind controllers, and
            <a href="/xbox-cloud-gaming-phone-controller/">it is explained here</a>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Steam, emulators, browser games</h3>
          <p>
            Anything reading XInput, DirectInput, GameInput or the browser
            Gamepad API sees an ordinary Xbox pad. No per-game configuration.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Nothing leaves your network</h3>
          <p>
            No account, no server, no telemetry. The phone talks to your PC
            directly over your own Wi-Fi, and every packet is authenticated so
            nothing else on the network can inject input.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">Sticks that behave like sticks</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="muted">
          Most on-screen controllers feel dead at the start of the travel and
          then far too sharp. That is two deadzones stacked on top of each
          other. A physical stick needs an inner deadzone because it has spring
          slop and sensor drift to hide, and the game at the far end already
          applies one on that assumption. Add another in the app and the first
          quarter of your thumb's travel does nothing at all.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          PhonePad does the opposite. The moment your thumb leaves centre, the
          output starts just past the game's own dead band, and the rest of your
          travel is spent on the range the game actually responds to.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="table-scroll" style="max-width:640px;margin-top:2rem">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Thumb travel</th>
              <th>Typical app</th>
              <th>PhonePad</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>5%</td><td>nothing</td><td>1.2%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>10%</td><td>nothing</td><td>4.3%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>20%</td><td>nothing</td><td>12.3%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>30%</td><td>6.4%</td><td>21.9%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>50%</td><td>33.2%</td><td>44.2%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>100%</td><td>100%</td><td>100%</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="small muted">
        What the game receives, composed with a typical in-game deadzone of
        0.15. Measured on real hardware.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">How it works</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Run the companion on your PC</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            A single self-contained program. No installer, no .NET. It creates
            the virtual controller Windows can see.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Open the app and tap your PC</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            Your PC appears on its own within a second or two. Type the six
            digits it shows, once, ever.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Play</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            After the first pairing, opening the app and tapping your PC goes
            straight to the controller.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p style="margin-top:2rem">
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/setup/">Full setup guide</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">What it looks like</h2>
      <p class="lede">Real screenshots, from a real phone.</p>
      <figure class="shot-wide">
        <img
          src="/shots/controller.jpg"
          width="1200"
          height="554"
          alt="The PhonePad controller in landscape: two sticks, a D-pad, four face buttons, shoulder buttons and analogue triggers, drawn as thin white outlines over a near-black background"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          The controller. Monochrome and outline-first on purpose: it is drawn
          over your game, not instead of it.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="shots">
        <figure>
          <img
            src="/shots/onboarding.jpg"
            width="560"
            height="1133"
            alt="The PhonePad first-run screen reading: No controller? Use your phone."
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>First run explains that a PC component exists.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <img
            src="/shots/connect.jpg"
            width="560"
            height="1133"
            alt="The connect screen showing a PC found on the network, ready to pair"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Your PC appears on its own. One tap to pair, then one tap to play.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <img
            src="/shots/controller-portrait.jpg"
            width="560"
            height="1133"
            alt="The PhonePad controller in portrait orientation"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Portrait is a separate arrangement, switched automatically.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">Make it yours</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="muted">
          Nothing about the on-screen pad is fixed. Move, resize, rotate and
          hide any control; tune deadzone, sensitivity and response per stick;
          keep separate arrangements for landscape and portrait that switch when
          you rotate the phone.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          Four presets ship: <strong>Standard</strong>, <strong>Racing</strong>
          with a larger left stick and analogue triggers,
          <strong>Precision</strong> for aiming, and <strong>Southpaw</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">Open source, and staying that way</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="muted">
          PhonePad is GPL-3.0. The whole thing is on GitHub: the Flutter app,
          the Rust companion, the wire protocol, the tests and the measurements
          behind every number on this page.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div style="display:flex;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:2.5rem">
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={REPO} rel="noopener">Read the source</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={`${REPO}/issues`} rel="noopener">Report a bug</a>
      </div>
      <Support />
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero-section {
    padding-top: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
    padding-bottom: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .hero {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4rem);
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.2fr);
    align-items: center;
  }
  .hero h1 {
    margin-bottom: 0.4em;
  }
  .cta {
    display: flex;
    gap: 12px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  /*
   * The one authored moment on the page. The brand's light, the same three
   * stops as the app icon, sits behind the phone and drifts very slowly. It is
   * the only place on the site the gradient appears, because it is the only
   * place the mark's world is being shown rather than described.
   */
  .hero-art {
    position: relative;
    padding: clamp(24px, 6vw, 56px);
  }
  .light {
    position: absolute;
    inset: -22%;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background:
      radial-gradient(40% 40% at 24% 76%, var(--light-1) 0%, transparent 100%),
      radial-gradient(34% 34% at 76% 22%, var(--light-3) 0%, transparent 100%),
      radial-gradient(52% 48% at 50% 50%, var(--light-2) 0%, transparent 100%);
    filter: blur(36px) saturate(1.05);
    opacity: 0.9;
    animation: drift 24s ease-in-out infinite alternate;
    will-change: transform;
  }
  @keyframes drift {
    from {
      transform: rotate(-4deg) scale(1);
    }
    to {
      transform: rotate(5deg) scale(1.06);
    }
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .light {
      animation: none;
    }
  }

  .phone {
    position: relative;
    background: var(--night);
    border-radius: 36px;
    padding: 10px;
    box-shadow: 0 30px 60px -24px rgba(21, 20, 43, 0.5);
  }

  .steps {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0;
    max-width: 760px;
    counter-reset: step;
  }
  .steps li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 56px 1fr;
    gap: 16px;
    padding: 22px 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    counter-increment: step;
  }
  .steps li:last-child {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .steps li::before {
    content: counter(step);
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: 2rem;
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    color: var(--brand);
  }
  .steps h3 {
    grid-column: 2;
    margin: 0 0 6px;
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }
  .steps p {
    grid-column: 2;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .tint {
    background: var(--sunken);
  }

  .shot-wide {
    margin: 2.4rem 0 0;
  }
  .shot-wide img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: var(--r-l);
    display: block;
  }

  .shots {
    display: grid;
    gap: 28px;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr));
    margin-top: 2.4rem;
  }
  .shots img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: var(--r-l);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    display: block;
  }

  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .hero {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
    .hero-art {
      padding: 20px 8px;
    }
    .steps li {
      grid-template-columns: 40px 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
